 "2c2387a4-202d-528a-b0ca-3f87d5a06ea3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078511 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078511 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186079214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078511 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186079218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078511 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186526018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078511 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186526039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078511 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Graph Transformations Resulting From Three Operations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078511 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186078361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078511 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "17339421-a1ad-554f-aed8-28e20cebab10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078512 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186079217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078512 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186079221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078512 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078512 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186526043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078512 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Graph Transformations Resulting From Four Operations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078512 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186078362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078512 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d69a3ef2-0854-503b-8586-b0113966d9ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078658 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078658 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078658 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078658 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078658 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078658 #edb/ref 1045 "Combining Graph Transformations: Three or More Operations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078658 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186079423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078658 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078658 #edb/ref 1050 780.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078658 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "ff014b49-75da-5266-b4b5-9e7bd3e5b3d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079219 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186078092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079219 #edb/ref 1069 "q-87488" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079219 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079219 #edb/ref 1071 "The graph of $y=4f(2x)-3,$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by applying **some** of the following transformations:\n\n- $(\\text{T}1)$ - Translate up by $3$ units\n\n- $(\\text{T}2)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}3)$ - Translate down by $3$ units\n\n- $(\\text{T}4)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $0.5$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\nWhich of the following could be a possible ordering of the appropriate transformations?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079219 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0e1845bd-ab9c-5876-9015-5e45dd5e00ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079219 #edb/ref 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo get $y=4f(2x)-3,$ we could apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}4).$ Stretch $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}=0.5$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(2x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}2).$ Stretch $y=f(2x)$ by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=4f(2x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}3).$ Translate $y=4f(2x)$ down by $3$ units to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=4f(2x)-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a possible order of transformations is\n\n$\\,$ $(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079423 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079423 #edb/ref 1061 28409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079423 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e6e20122-6866-5fbc-9527-7a17b8c290e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079423 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186079675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079424 #edb/ref 1061 28411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079424 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a78dc881-a3e2-5e40-bd1b-cf28fe897545" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079424 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079425 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079425 #edb/ref 1061 28412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079425 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a6368e4f-8808-5bd9-b6a8-0a2f253f3384" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079425 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079426 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079426 #edb/ref 1061 28413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079426 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6856acd9-d60c-536e-80e4-da84599cde69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079426 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079675 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we are given the graph of ![](images/c9/c956e6cbfb2d94fa56a68a3173ec78f60fee33a9066c9c44ca4096d67d7b1cba.svg) shown below.\n\n![](images/2c/2cb61ee785ff6e04c5b73281389d6653f42bc93636da6840fbc2391ffb82451c.png)\n\nHow can we use graph transformations to plot the graph of\n\n![](images/c5/c546f9b96ef07d40050b7d98f046528f54ea43afc5d42c1bac48d2a02406e079.svg)\n\nSince this function involves several transformations, we apply them in the following order:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of ![](images/a0/a047a10a3ed3f5e86676e8fca21093abfe6a1849d90ce94b62101f