hes, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny=7f(2x-2)+5=7f(2(x-1))+5\n$$\n\nTo get $y=7f(2(x-1))+5,$ we could apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}4).$ Stretch $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}=0.5$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(2x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}3).$ Translate $y=f(2x)$ right by $1$ unit to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(2(x-1)).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}2).$ Stretch $y=f(2(x-1))$ by a scale factor of $7$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=7f(2(x-1)).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}1).$ Translate $y=7f(2(x-1))$ up by $5$ units to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=7f(2(x-1))+5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a possible order of transformations is\n\n$\\,$ $(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}3)\\to (\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549039 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549039 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112955" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549039 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549039 #edb/ref 1071 "The graph of $y=3f(5x-15),$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by applying **some** of the following transformations:\n\n- $(\\text{T}1)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}2)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $0.2$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}3)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $5$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}4)$ - Translate right by $3$ units\n\nWhich of the following could be a possible ordering of the appropriate transformations?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549039 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a9454265-8028-5150-af82-6933624c324f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549039 #edb/ref 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny=3f(5x-15)=3f(5(x-3))\n$$\n\nTo get $y=3f(5(x-3)),$ we could apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}2).$ Stretch $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{5}=0.2$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(5x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}4).$ Translate $y=f(5x)$ right by $3$ units to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(5(x-3)).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}1).$ Stretch $y=f(5(x-3))$ by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=3f(5(x-3)).\n$$\n\nTherefore, a possible order of transformations is\n\n$\\,$ $(\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549079 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186549154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549079 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "10c35e87-699a-5a06-ba8b-912ea39e334d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549079 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186079219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549124 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186549079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549124 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8ee2a0af-2eaa-5542-8abb-4d17722ea629" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549124 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186549005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549154 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f9c0e085-a181-5a3a-a5b2-0ef40761da79" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549154 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186549039 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
